    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A federation of French colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, governed from Saint-Louis and from 1902 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> by a governor-general.</li><li>It ruled most Africans as subjects under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigenat">indigénat</a> code, with forced labour, head tax and conscription into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirailleurs">tirailleurs</a>.</li><li>Post-war reforms shifted power to the territories, and the federation dissolved in 1958–1959 as eight states moved to independence.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Despite their name, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirailleurs">tirailleurs</a> sénégalais were recruited from across French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, and many came from present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

