    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe.jpg" alt="Louis Faidherbe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Louis Faidherbe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faidherbearmee.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie Madeleine Rignot-Dubaux, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> (<em>Afrique occidentale française</em>, AOF) was created to bring order to the territories <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> had conquered in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in the late nineteenth century. France had held Saint-Louis and Gorée on the Senegalese coast since the seventeenth century, and from the 1850s Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Louis Faidherbe</a> expanded the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a>. In the 1880s and 1890s French columns pushed east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toucouleur">Tukulor</a> state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadu-tall">Ahmadu</a>, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> in 1894 and fighting a long war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Ture</a>, who was captured in 1898, while other expeditions conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey</a> in 1892–1894 and occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> kingdoms in 1896–1897. Separate colonies were set up on the coast in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> and Dahomey. A decree of 16 June 1895 placed Senegal, French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, French Guinea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">Ivory</a> Coast under a governor-general, who was also governor of Senegal at Saint-Louis, to coordinate military and economic policy. The early arrangement was loose, and the federation took its definitive shape under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-roume">Ernest Roume</a>, governor-general from 1902 to 1908: the seat of government moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a>, and a decree of 18 October 1904 gave the governor-general his own administration and a federal budget financed by customs duties. Dahomey was attached at the end of the 1890s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, Niger and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-volta">Upper Volta</a> were added as they were organised.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/conakry"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conakry.jpg" alt="Conakry" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/conakry">Conakry</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ville_de_Conaky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Boubacar Bila Diao Balde, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The federation covered about 4.7 million square kilometres, from the Atlantic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>, with a population of well over ten million that approached twenty million by the late 1950s. Its economy was organised around the export of a few crops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> became one of the world’s leading groundnut producers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> developed cocoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> exported palm products, and French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, including on the irrigated schemes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/office-du-niger">Office du Niger</a>, founded in 1932. Large French trading houses such as the Compagnie française de l’Afrique occidentale and the Société commerciale de l’Ouest africain dominated import and export trade, with Lebanese and Syrian traders as intermediaries. Railways were built from the ports into the interior, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> line, completed in the 1920s, and lines from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conakry">Conakry</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abidjan">Abidjan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotonou">Cotonou</a>. To pay the head tax imposed on adults, Africans grew cash crops or migrated for wage labour, while forced labour was used for roads, railways and plantations. Education reached only a small minority: a network of village and regional schools fed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecole-william-ponty">École normale William Ponty</a>, which trained teachers, clerks and medical assistants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread widely under colonial rule, carried by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> brotherhoods such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mouride-brotherhood">Mouride</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tijaniyya">Tijani orders</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/volta-bani-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/volta-bani-war.jpg" alt="Volta-Bani War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/volta-bani-war">Volta-Bani War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_de_l%27Afrique-Occidentale_fran%C3%A7aise,_1914.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hogweard, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The federation rested on armed force. Its main army was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirailleurs">tirailleurs</a> sénégalais, African infantry regiments founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Faidherbe</a> in 1857 and recruited, despite the name, from across French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> under French officers. They completed the conquest of the interior, suppressed revolts and garrisoned the colonies, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> was developed as a major naval base and the headquarters of French forces in West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Resistance continued long after the formal conquest: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-bani-war">Bani–Volta war</a> of 1915–1916 in the west of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> revolts of 1916–1917 led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaocen-ag-mohammed">Kaocen</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and Firhoun in present-day Mali, were among the largest. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">Conscription</a>, introduced in 1912, provided between about 160,000 and 200,000 soldiers for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, many of them recruited in 1918 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blaise-diagne">Blaise Diagne</a>, the first black African elected to the French Chamber of Deputies, in return for promises of reform. Tirailleurs fought again in 1939–1940 and in the Free French campaigns from 1943. In September 1940 the Vichy authorities under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-boisson">Pierre Boisson</a> repelled a British and Free French attack on Dakar, and the federation joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> only after the landings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in November 1942. On 1 December 1944 French troops fired on repatriated tirailleurs demanding back pay at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiaroye-massacre">Thiaroye</a> camp near Dakar, killing at least thirty-five by the official count; many historians believe the toll was higher.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/blaise-diagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/blaise-diagne.jpg" alt="Blaise Diagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/blaise-diagne">Blaise Diagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blaise_Diagne-1921.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agence Meurisse (domaine public, voir fiche détaillée Gallica), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the head of the federation stood the governor-general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a>, appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and responsible to the Ministry of the Colonies. He controlled the federal budget, customs, public works, railways, health services and defence, and supervised lieutenant-governors who ran each colony. Below them the territory was divided into <em>cercles</em> under French administrators, the <em>commandants de cercle</em>, who governed through African chiefs, many appointed for their loyalty rather than their traditional standing and charged with collecting taxes and recruiting labour and soldiers. Most Africans were legally subjects (<em>sujets</em>) rather than citizens, governed under the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigenat">indigénat</a></em>, a code that allowed administrators to impose fines and imprisonment without trial. A small minority, the <em>originaires</em> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-communes">Four Communes</a> of Saint-Louis, Gorée, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rufisque">Rufisque</a> and Dakar, held French citizenship, confirmed by laws of 1915 and 1916 sponsored by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blaise-diagne">Blaise Diagne</a>, and elected a deputy to Paris. The official ideology was the ‘civilising mission’, which combined assimilation in principle with rule through chiefs in practice. Governors-general such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-roume">Ernest Roume</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-merlaud-ponty">William Merlaud-Ponty</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joost-van-vollenhoven">Joost van Vollenhoven</a> shaped the system. After 1946 the indigénat and forced labour were abolished, all Africans became citizens, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-of-french-west-africa">Grand Council of French West Africa</a>, elected by the territorial assemblies, gave Africans a voice in federal affairs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bamako"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bamako.jpg" alt="Bamako" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Place_de_la_libert%C3%A9_-_Bamako.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rgaudin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> shaped the map and institutions of eight modern states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, whose borders largely follow the internal boundaries drawn by the federation. French became the language of government and education, and the legal, administrative and school systems of these countries still bear its imprint. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cfa-franc">CFA franc</a>, created in 1945 for the French colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, survives in reformed form as the West African CFA franc, linking several of the successor states to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> through monetary cooperation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> grew into a major Atlantic port and a cosmopolitan capital whose university, founded in 1957, became a centre of African scholarship. The federation also produced a remarkable political and intellectual elite. Graduates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecole-william-ponty">École William Ponty</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibo-keita">Modibo Keïta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamani-diori">Hamani Diori</a>, led their countries to independence; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Léopold Sédar Senghor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-houphouet-boigny">Félix Houphouët-Boigny</a> sat in the French parliament and served in governments of the Fourth Republic; and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rassemblement-democratique-africain">Rassemblement Démocratique Africain</a>, founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> in 1946, became the first African mass party organised across several territories. The service of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirailleurs">tirailleurs</a> sénégalais in two world wars, and the massacre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thiaroye-massacre">Thiaroye</a>, remain important in the historical memory of West Africa and France.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-world-war.jpg" alt="Second World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_101I-646-5188-17,_Flugzeuge_Junkers_Ju_87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Opitz, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The federation was undone by the political reforms that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville-conference">Brazzaville Conference</a> of 1944 promised change, and the constitution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> in 1946 gave Africans citizenship and representation in the French parliament, while new parties, trade unions and a growing press challenged colonial rule; the strike of 1947–1948 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> railway showed their strength. The framework law of 23 June 1956, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loi-cadre-defferre">loi-cadre Defferre</a></em>, introduced universal suffrage and gave each territory an elected assembly and its own executive council, shifting power from Dakar to the territorial capitals. Leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-sedar-senghor">Senghor</a> denounced this as ‘balkanisation’, while Houphouët-Boigny, whose wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> subsidised poorer territories through the federal budget, welcomed it. In the referendum of 28 September 1958 on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-community">French Community</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmed-sekou-toure">Ahmed Sékou Touré</a> voted no and became independent on 2 October 1958; the other territories voted yes and became autonomous republics within the Community. The federal government-general was wound up in 1958–1959. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> briefly formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-federation">Mali Federation</a>, which broke apart in August 1960, and by the end of 1960 all the former territories of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> were independent states.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> was the federation through which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ruled most of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> for six decades, shaping the borders, languages, economies and elites of eight present-day states, from the forced labour and conscription of the colonial era to the reforms that led to independence in 1958–1960.</em></p>

